"""Deterministic restoration for the Extended Unit I answer.

NotebookLM renders this answer's mathematics without KaTeX annotation nodes
(``latex: []`` in the export), so Chrome copy drops every bullet list that
contains math and flattens the surviving formulas.  When the distinctive
heading is present but the LaTeX environments are missing, replace that part
of the answer with the known-good source so the worked examples and vectors
survive a fresh re-export.

This mirrors the existing gate-verification/Hadamard repairs: narrow trigger,
no model call, and no duplication when the answer is already intact.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import re


EXTENDED_MARKER = "UNIT - I (Extended): Foundational Mathematics, Physics, and Biology"

# Chrome's copy can break the title across lines, so match the words rather
# than one exact string. An optional leading Markdown heading is included in
# the replacement so the restored source does not become ``## # UNIT ...``.
_MARKER_RE = re.compile(
    r"UNIT[ \t]*-[ \t]*I[ \t]*\(Extended\)[ \t]*:"
    r"[ \t]*Foundational\s+Mathematics\s*,\s*Physics\s*,\s*and\s+Biology"
)
_NEXT_STEPS_RE = re.compile(r"(?mi)^[^\n]*\bNext Steps\b[^\n]*$")
_HEADING_PREFIX_RE = re.compile(r"^[ \t]{0,3}#{1,6}[ \t]+$")

# Minimum number of real LaTeX environments in an intact copy.  The mangled
# export has none; a correct copy from the notes has many.
_INTACT_MIN_MATRICES = 8

_EXTENDED_ANSWER = '# UNIT - I (Extended): Foundational Mathematics, Physics, and Biology\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1: BACKGROUND MATHEMATICS [10 Marks]\n\n### 1. Basics of Linear Algebra (3.5 Marks)\nLinear algebra provides the mathematical language of quantum information theory. All state spaces in quantum computation are modeled as finite-dimensional complex inner product spaces.\n\n#### **A. Dirac Bra-Ket Notation and State Vectors**\n* **Ket Vector (\\\\(|\\psi\\rangle\\\\))**: A state vector in a complex vector space \\\\(\\mathbb{C}^d\\\\) represented as a column vector.\n  \\\\[|0\\rangle = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad |1\\rangle = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\\\]\n* **Bra Vector (\\\\(\\langle\\psi|\\\\))**: The dual vector to \\\\(|\\psi\\rangle\\\\) in the dual space \\\\(\\mathcal{H}^*\\\\), defined by taking the conjugate transpose (Hermitian adjoint) \\\\((|\\psi\\rangle)^\\dagger = (\\mathbf{\\bar{\\psi}})^T\\\\):\n  \\\\[\\langle 0| = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\langle 1| = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\\\]\n\n#### **B. Vector and Operator Multiplication**\n1. **Inner Product (\\\\(\\langle\\varphi|\\psi\\rangle\\\\))**: Maps two vectors to a complex scalar, representing geometric overlap and projection.\n   \\\\[\\\\langle 0|0\\\\rangle = \\\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\end{bmatrix} \\\\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\\\\\ 0 \\\\end{bmatrix} = 1, \\\\quad \\\\langle 0|1\\\\rangle = \\\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\end{bmatrix} \\\\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\\\\\ 1 \\\\end{bmatrix} = 0\\\\]\n   * **Conjugate Symmetry**: \\\\(\\langle\\varphi|\\psi\\rangle = \\langle\\psi|\\varphi\\rangle^*\\\\).\n   * **Vector Norm**: \\\\(\\||\\psi\\rangle\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle\\psi|\\psi\\rangle}\\\\). If \\\\(\\||\\psi\\rangle\\| = 1\\\\), it is a **unit vector**.\n   * **Worked Dual Computation**: Given \\\\(|\\alpha\\rangle = i|0\\rangle + 7|1\\rangle\\\\) and \\\\(|\\beta\\rangle = 3|0\\rangle + |1\\rangle\\\\), the dual vector is \\\\(\\langle\\alpha| = -i\\langle 0| + 7\\langle 1|\\\\). \n     \\\\[\\langle\\alpha|\\beta\\rangle = (-i\\langle 0| + 7\\langle 1|)(3|0\\rangle + |1\\rangle) = -3i\\langle 0|0\\rangle + 7\\langle 1|1\\rangle = -3i + 7\\\\]\n\n2. **Outer Product (\\\\(|\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\varphi|\\\\))**: Maps vectors to linear operators (matrices):\n   \\\\[|0\\rangle\\langle 0| = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad |0\\rangle\\langle 1| = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}\\\\]\n   \\\\[|1\\rangle\\langle 0| = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad |1\\rangle\\langle 1| = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\\\]\n\n3. **Tensor Product (\\\\(\\otimes\\\\) / Kronecker Product)**: Combines vector spaces to represent composite quantum systems:\n   \\\\[|0\\rangle \\otimes |1\\rangle = |01\\rangle = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\otimes \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}\\\\]\n\n#### **C. Special Matrix Classes**\n* **Hermitian Matrices**: Operators satisfying \\\\(A^\\dagger = A\\\\) (where \\\\(A^\\dagger = (A^T)^*\\\\)). They possess real eigenvalues and represent physical observables.\n* **Unitary Matrices**: Operators satisfying \\\\(U^\\dagger U = U U^\\dagger = I\\\\) (or \\\\(U^\\dagger = U^{-1}\\\\)). They describe norm-preserving quantum evolutions and reversible logic gates.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Hilbert Space (3.5 Marks)\nA **Hilbert space** (\\\\(\\mathcal{H}\\\\)) is a complex inner product space that is complete under the vector norm induced by its inner product.\n\n#### **A. Postulate 1: State Space Formalism**\nPostulate 1 dictates that any isolated physical system is associated with a complex Hilbert space \\\\(\\mathcal{H}\\\\). The state of the system is completely specified by its state vector \\\\(|\\psi\\rangle\\\\), which is a normalized unit vector in \\\\(\\mathcal{H}\\\\):\n\\\\[|\\psi\\rangle = \\alpha|0\\rangle + \\beta|1\\rangle \\quad \\text{such that} \\quad \\langle\\psi|\\psi\\rangle = |\\alpha|^2 + |\\beta|^2 = 1\\\\]\n\n```text\n                           Hilbert Space C²\n                    ┌────────────────────────────┐\n                    │      State Vector |ψ⟩      │\n                    │   |ψ⟩ = α|0⟩ + β|1⟩        │\n                    │                            │\n                    │  Normalization Constraint: │\n                    │     ⟨ψ|ψ⟩ = |α|²+|β|² = 1  │\n                    └──────────────┬─────────────┘\n                                   │\n                    ┌──────────────┴─────────────┐\n                    │ Orthonormal Basis {|0⟩,|1⟩}│\n                    │ ⟨0|0⟩ = 1, ⟨1|1⟩ = 1       │\n                    │ ⟨0|1⟩ = 0 (Orthogonal)     │\n                    └────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n#### **B. Orthonormal Bases and Completeness Relation**\n* **Orthonormality**: A basis \\\\(\\{|i\\rangle\\}\\\\) is orthonormal if all basis vectors are unit vectors and mutually orthogonal:\n  \\\\[\\langle i|j\\rangle = \\delta_{ij} = \\begin{cases} 1 & \\text{if } i = j \\\\ 0 & \\text{if } i \\neq j \\end{cases}\\\\]\n* **Completeness Relation**: The outer products of an orthonormal basis sum to the identity operator \\\\(I\\\\):\n  \\\\[\\sum_{i} |i\\rangle\\langle i| = I \\implies |0\\rangle\\langle 0| + |1\\rangle\\langle 1| = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = I\\\\]\n  This relation allows any arbitrary operator \\\\(A\\\\) to be expanded in terms of matrix elements \\\\(\\langle i|A|j\\rangle\\\\):\n  \\\\[A = I A I = \\sum_{i,j} |i\\rangle\\langle i|A|j\\rangle\\langle j| = \\sum_{i,j} \\langle i|A|j\\rangle |i\\rangle\\langle j|\\\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3. Probabilities and Measurements (3.0 Marks)\n\n#### **A. The Born Rule**\nThe Born rule states that when a quantum system prepared in state \\\\(|\\psi\\rangle\\\\) is measured in an orthonormal basis \\\\(\\{|x\\rangle\\}\\\\), the probability \\\\(P(x)\\\\) of obtaining outcome \\\\(x\\\\) is given by the squared magnitude of the inner product projection:\n\\\\[P(x) = |\\langle x|\\psi\\rangle|^2\\\\]\n\n#### **B. Postulate 3: General Measurement Operators (\\\\(\\{M_m\\\\}\\\\))**\nQuantum measurements are defined by a collection of measurement operators \\\\(\\{M_m\\}\\\\) acting on \\\\(\\mathcal{H}\\\\).\n* **Completeness Equation**: Probabilities must sum to 1:\n  \\\\[\\sum_m M_m^\\dagger M_m = I \\implies \\sum_m P(m) = \\sum_m \\langle\\psi|M_m^\\dagger M_m|\\psi\\rangle = 1\\\\]\n* **State Collapse**: Upon measuring outcome \\\\(m\\\\), the post-measurement state vector collapses to:\n  \\\\[|\\psi\'\\rangle = \\frac{M_m |\\psi\\rangle}{\\sqrt{\\langle\\psi|M_m^\\dagger M_m|\\psi\\rangle}} = \\frac{M_m |\\psi\\rangle}{\\sqrt{P(m)}}\\\\]\n\n#### **C. Projective (Von Neumann) Measurements vs. POVMs**\n1. **Projective Measurements**: Special case where measurement operators are orthogonal projectors \\\\(P_m = P_m^\\dagger = P_m^2\\\\) satisfying \\\\(P_m P_{m\'} = \\delta_{m,m\'} P_m\\\\). An observable is a Hermitian matrix \\\\(M = \\sum_m m P_m\\\\), where \\\\(m\\\\) represents the measurement eigenvalues.\n2. **Positive Operator-Valued Measures (POVMs)**: Used when only measurement statistics matter. Defined by positive operators \\\\(E_m \\equiv M_m^\\dagger M_m \\ge 0\\\\) satisfying \\\\(\\sum_m E_m = I\\\\). POVMs enable unambiguous state discrimination between non-orthogonal quantum states without making errors.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2: BACKGROUND PHYSICS [10 Marks]\n\n### 1. Basics of Quantum Mechanics and Pauli\'s Exclusion Principle (2.5 Marks)\n\n#### **A. The Standard Model and Particle Classification**\nAll elementary particles in the universe are classified into two fundamental families based on their intrinsic angular momentum (**spin**):\n\n```text\n                                  Standard Model Particles\n                                ┌────────────┴────────────┐\n                                │                         │\n                        Fermions (Spin-1/2)       Bosons (Integer Spin)\n                        ┌───────┴───────┐         ┌───────┴───────┐\n                        │               │         │               │\n                     Quarks          Leptons    Photons        Gluons, W/Z,\n                  (Up, Down...)   (Electron...) (Spin-1)     Higgs (Spin-0)\n```\n\n* **Fermions (Half-Integer Spin \\\\(1/2, 3/2, \\dots\\\\))**: Include quarks (up, down, charm, strange, top, bottom) and leptons (electron, muon, tau, and their neutrinos) [6, 7, 55–58, 229].\n* **Bosons (Integer Spin \\\\(0, 1, 2, \\dots\\\\))**: Include gauge force carriers such as photons (electromagnetic force), gluons (strong nuclear force), \\\\(W/Z\\\\) bosons (weak nuclear force), and the scalar Higgs boson (spin-0).\n\n#### **B. Pauli\'s Exclusion Principle**\n* **Antisymmetric Wavefunctions**: A multi-particle state vector of identical fermions must be anti-symmetric under the permutation of any two particles:\n  \\\\[\\Psi(x_1, x_2) = -\\Psi(x_2, x_1)\\\\]\n* **The Exclusion Principle**: Two identical fermions cannot occupy the exact same quantum state simultaneously. If \\\\(x_1 = x_2\\\\), then \\\\(\\Psi(x_1, x_1) = -\\Psi(x_1, x_1) \\implies \\Psi = 0\\\\). This fundamental principle prevents matter from collapsing and dictates electronic shell structures in atoms and biomolecules.\n* **Bosonic Contrast**: Bosons possess symmetric wavefunctions (\\\\(\\Psi(x_1, x_2) = \\Psi(x_2, x_1)\\\\)) and can occupy identical quantum states, enabling lasers and Bose-Einstein condensates.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Superposition, Entanglement, and Bell\'s Inequalities (2.5 Marks)\n\n#### **A. Superposition**\nA quantum system can exist in a linear combination of all its basis states simultaneously. For a single qubit:\n\\\\[|\\psi\\rangle = \\alpha|0\\rangle + \\beta|1\\rangle\\\\]\nSuperposition allows quantum algorithms to process \\\\(2^n\\\\) states in parallel via Hadamard transformations.\n\n#### **B. Entanglement & Bell States**\nQuantum entanglement occurs when a composite state vector cannot be factored into a simple tensor product of individual qubit states:\n\\\\[|\\psi_{AB}\\rangle \\neq |\\psi_A\\rangle \\otimes |\\psi_B\\rangle\\\\]\n\nThe 4 maximally entangled orthonormal **Bell states** (EPR pairs) are generated by a circuit applying a Hadamard gate followed by a CNOT gate:\n\n```text\n  |i⟩ ────[ H ]────■──── |β_ij⟩\n                   │\n  |j⟩ ─────────────⊕────\n```\n\n1.  \\\\(|\\Phi^+\\rangle = \\frac{|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\) (from input \\\\(|00\\rangle\\\\))\n2.  \\\\(|\\Phi^-\\rangle = \\frac{|00\\rangle - |11\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\) (from input \\\\(|10\\rangle\\\\))\n3.  \\\\(|\\Psi^+\\rangle = \\frac{|01\\rangle + |10\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\) (from input \\\\(|01\\rangle\\\\))\n4.  \\\\(|\\Psi^-\\rangle = \\frac{|01\\rangle - |10\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\) (from input \\\\(|11\\rangle\\\\))\n\n#### **C. Bell\'s Inequality Violation**\nEinstein, Podolsky, and Rosen (EPR) argued that quantum mechanics was incomplete and governed by "local hidden variables" [178–181]. John Bell proved that any local realistic theory must satisfy the **Clauser-Horne-Shimony-Holt (CHSH) inequality** [181–183]:\n\\\\[|\\langle QS \\rangle + \\langle RS \\rangle + \\langle RT \\rangle - \\langle QT \\rangle| \\le 2\\\\]\nBy evaluating the Bell singlet state \\\\(|\\Psi^-\\rangle = \\frac{|01\\rangle - |10\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\), quantum mechanics predicts a value of \\\\(2\\sqrt{2} \\approx 2.828 > 2\\\\). Experiments confirm this violation, proving that nature is inherently non-local.\n\n#### **D. Supersymmetry (SUSY)**\nSupersymmetry is a theoretical extension of quantum field theory predicting a fundamental symmetry between fermions and bosons, pairing every fermion with a bosonic superpartner (e.g., selectrons, squarks) and vice versa.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Density Operators and Quantum Correlations (2.5 Marks)\n\n#### **A. Pure vs. Mixed States**\n* **Pure State**: Complete knowledge of the system is available, described by a single state vector \\\\(|\\psi\\rangle\\\\) with density operator \\\\(\\rho = |\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi|\\\\).\n  * **Purity Criterion**: \\\\(\\text{tr}(\\rho^2) = 1\\\\).\n* **Mixed State**: Represents a statistical ensemble \\\\(\\{p_i, |\\psi_i\\rangle\\\\}\\\\), where the system is in state \\\\(|\\psi_i\\rangle\\\\) with classical probability \\\\(p_i\\\\):\n  \\\\[\\rho = \\sum_i p_i |\\psi_i\\rangle\\langle\\psi_i| \\quad \\text{where} \\quad p_i \\ge 0, \\quad \\sum_i p_i = 1\\\\]\n  * **Purity Criterion**: \\\\(\\text{tr}(\\rho^2) < 1\\\\).\n\n#### **B. Fundamental Properties of Density Matrices**\n1. **Hermiticity**: \\\\(\\rho^\\dagger = \\rho\\\\).\n2. **Unit Trace**: \\\\(\\text{tr}(\\rho) = \\sum_i p_i = 1\\\\).\n3. **Positivity**: \\\\(\\langle\\varphi|\\rho|\\varphi\\rangle \\ge 0\\\\) for all vectors \\\\(|\\varphi\\rangle\\\\).\n\n#### **C. Bloch Vector for Mixed Single-Qubit States**\nAn arbitrary single-qubit density matrix can be written using Pauli matrices:\n\\\\[\\rho = \\frac{I + \\vec{r} \\cdot \\vec{\\sigma}}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{bmatrix} 1 + r_z & r_x - i r_y \\\\ r_x + i r_y & 1 - r_z \\end{bmatrix}\\\\]\nwhere \\\\(\\vec{r} = (r_x, r_y, r_z)\\\\) is the **Bloch vector**.\n* Pure state \\\\(\\iff \\|\\vec{r}\\| = 1\\\\) (surface of the Bloch sphere).\n* Mixed state \\\\(\\iff \\|\\vec{r}\\| < 1\\\\) (interior of the Bloch sphere).\n* Completely mixed state (maximum entropy) \\\\(\\implies \\rho = \\frac{I}{2} \\implies \\vec{r} = \\mathbf{0}\\\\) (center of the sphere).\n\n#### **D. Partial Trace and Reduced Density Operators**\nFor a composite system \\\\(AB\\\\) in joint state \\\\(\\rho^{AB}\\\\), the subsystem \\\\(A\\\\) is described by the **reduced density operator** \\\\(\\rho^A\\\\), obtained by taking the **partial trace** over system \\\\(B\\\\):\n\\\\[\\rho^A = \\text{tr}_B(\\rho^{AB})\\\\]\nFor the Bell state \\\\(|\\Phi^+\\rangle = \\frac{|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\):\n\\\\[\\rho^{AB} = \\frac{1}{2} \\Big( |00\\rangle\\langle 00| + |00\\rangle\\langle 11| + |11\\rangle\\langle 00| + |11\\rangle\\langle 11| \\Big)\\\\]\nTracing out system \\\\(B\\\\) yields a completely mixed reduced state for qubit \\\\(A\\\\):\n\\\\[\\rho^A = \\text{tr}_B(\\rho^{AB}) = \\frac{1}{2} |0\\rangle\\langle 0| + \\frac{1}{2} |1\\rangle\\langle 1| = \\begin{bmatrix} 1/2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1/2 \\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{I}{2}\\\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4. Measurements in Non-Computational Bases (2.5 Marks)\n\nQuantum mechanics allows measuring qubits in any arbitrary orthonormal basis \\\\(\\{|a\\rangle, |b\\rangle\\\\}\\\\) besides the standard computational basis \\\\(\\{|0\\rangle, |1\\rangle\\\\}\\\\).\n\n#### **A. Measuring in the Hadamard / Diagonal Basis (\\\\(\\{|+\\rangle, |-\\rangle\\\\}\\\\))**\nThe diagonal basis vectors are:\n\\\\[|+\\rangle = \\frac{|0\\rangle + |1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}, \\quad |-\\rangle = \\frac{|0\\rangle - |1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\]\nExpressed in terms of the diagonal basis, computational states are:\n\\\\[|0\\rangle = \\frac{|+\\rangle + |-\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}, \\quad |1\\rangle = \\frac{|+\\rangle - |-\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\]\n\n#### **B. Worked Example: Measuring a Superposition State**\nGiven the state \\\\(|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}|0\\rangle + \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}|1\\rangle\\\\):\n1. **Measurement in Computational Basis (\\\\(\\{|0\\rangle, |1\\rangle\\\\}\\\\))**:\n   \\\\[P(0) = |\\langle 0|\\psi\\rangle|^2 = \\left|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right|^2 = \\frac{1}{3}, \\quad P(1) = |\\langle 1|\\psi\\rangle|^2 = \\left|\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\right|^2 = \\frac{2}{3}\\\\]\n2. **Measurement in Diagonal Basis (\\\\(\\{|+\\rangle, |-\\rangle\\\\}\\\\))**:\n   Substitute \\\\(|0\\rangle\\\\) and \\\\(|1\\rangle\\\\) into \\\\(|\\psi\\rangle\\\\):\n   \\\\[|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\left(\\frac{|+\\rangle + |-\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) + \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\left(\\frac{|+\\rangle - |-\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)|+\\rangle + \\left(\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)|-\\rangle\\\\]\n   * \\\\(P(+) = \\left|\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right|^2 = \\frac{1 + 2\\sqrt{2} + 2}{6} = \\frac{3 + 2\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\approx 0.971\\\\)\n   * \\\\(P(-) = \\left|\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right|^2 = \\frac{1 - 2\\sqrt{2} + 2}{6} = \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\approx 0.029\\\\)\n\n#### **C. Measuring Spin along an Arbitrary Axis \\\\(\\vec{v}\\\\)**\nFor any 3D unit vector \\\\(\\vec{v} = (v_1, v_2, v_3)\\\\), the spin observable is \\\\(\\vec{v} \\cdot \\vec{\\sigma} = v_1 X + v_2 Y + v_3 Z\\\\). The measurement projectors onto its \\\\(\\pm 1\\\\) eigenspaces are:\n\\\\[P_{\\pm} = \\frac{I \\pm \\vec{v} \\cdot \\vec{\\sigma}}{2}\\\\]\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3: BACKGROUND BIOLOGY [10 Marks]\n\n### 1. Basic Concepts of Genomics and Proteomics: The Central Dogma [10 Marks]\n\n#### **A. The Central Dogma of Molecular Biology**\nThe **Central Dogma** (formulated by Francis Crick) describes the directional flow of genetic information within a biological system:\n\n```text\n           Replication\n             ┌───┐\n             ▼   │\n          [  DNA  ] ───────── Transcription ─────────> [  RNA  ] ───────── Translation ─────────> [ Protein ]\n        (Genomics)                                    (mRNA)                                   (Proteomics)\n```\n\n1. **Replication**: DNA directs its own self-duplication during the S phase of the cell cycle, ensuring genetic inheritance across cellular divisions.\n2. **Transcription**: Genomic DNA in the cell nucleus is copied into single-stranded **messenger RNA (mRNA)**.\n3. **Translation**: mRNA migrates to the **ribosome**, where its genetic code is translated into a specific linear chain of amino acids, forming a functional protein.\n\n---\n\n#### **B. Genomics: Molecular Structure of DNA and Chromosomes (4.0 Marks)**\n* **Cellular Scale**: Human bodies contain \\\\(\\approx 37 \\times 10^{12}\\\\) cells. Each cell nucleus packages **23 pairs of chromosomes**. Human DNA contains \\\\(\\approx 3 \\times 10^9\\\\) (3 billion) base pairs.\n* **Chemical Structure of DNA**: DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid) stores genetic information using four nitrogenous bases:\n\n```text\n                                DNA Nitrogenous Bases\n                                ┌──────────┴──────────┐\n                        Purines (Double-Ring)     Pyrimidines (Single-Ring)\n                        ┌───────┴───────┐         ┌───────┴───────┐\n                        │               │         │               │\n                     Adenine         Guanine   Cytosine        Thymine\n                    (C₅H₅N₅)        (C₅H₅N₅O)  (C₅H₅N₃O)      (C₆H₃N₂O₂)\n```\n\n* **Complementary Base Pairing**: Bases form hydrogen bonds exclusively as **Adenine–Thymine (A–T)** and **Guanine–Cytosine (G–C)**.\n* **Nucleosides vs. Nucleotides**:\n  * **Nucleoside** = Base + Deoxyribose Sugar.\n  * **Nucleotide** = Base + Deoxyribose Sugar + Phosphate Group. Nucleotides form the structural backbone of the DNA double helix polymer.\n\n---\n\n#### **C. Proteomics: RNA, Translation, and Protein Function (3.5 Marks)**\n* **RNA Architecture**: RNA (Ribonucleic Acid) is single-stranded, contains ribose sugar, and replaces Thymine (T) with **Uracil (U)** (\\\\(\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_4\\text{N}_2\\text{O}_2\\\\)).\n* **Transcription Base Matching**: When transcribing DNA to mRNA, complementation maps bases as follows:\n  \\\\[\\text{DNA Input: } \\texttt{A - T - T - G - G - C - A - G} \\implies \\text{mRNA Output: } \\texttt{U - A - A - C - C - G - U - C}\\\\]\n* **Genetic Translation & Peptide Bonds**:\n  * **Codons**: A sequence of 3 consecutive mRNA bases forms a **codon**.\n  * **Anti-Codons**: Transfer RNA (tRNA) carries a complementary 3-base **anti-codon** attached to a specific amino acid.\n  * **Peptide Bond**: At the ribosome (rRNA), amino acids are linked sequentially via **peptide bonds** to form polypeptide chains.\n  \\\\[1 \\text{ Codon (mRNA)} + 1 \\text{ Anti-Codon (tRNA)} \\longrightarrow 1 \\text{ Amino Acid}\\\\]\n\n```text\n    DNA Strand:     --- A --- T --- G --- C --- A --- T ---\n                        │     │     │     │     │     │   (Transcription)\n    mRNA Codon:     --- U --- A --- C --- G --- U --- A ---\n                        │     │     │     │     │     │   (Translation at Ribosome)\n    tRNA Anti-Codon:--- A --- U --- G --- C --- A --- U ---\n                        └─────────────────┬─────────────────┘\n                                   1 Amino Acid\n                                         │  (Peptide Bond)\n                                   Protein Chain\n```\n\n* **Functional Classification of Proteins**:\n  1. **Enzymes**: Biological catalysts that are **manufactured and consumed at the same cellular location**.\n  2. **Hormones**: Signaling proteins that are **manufactured at one location and transported to be utilized at another target site**.\n\n---\n\n💡 **Next Steps**: Would you like to practice a set of sample exam numerical questions (such as calculating reduced density matrices, Bell state inner products, or base-pair measurement probabilities), or proceed to Unit II?\n'


def restore_extended_answer(text: str) -> str:
    """Restore worked examples/vectors when Chrome copy dropped the LaTeX."""
    match = _MARKER_RE.search(text)
    if match is None:
        return text
    if text[match.start():].count(r"\begin{bmatrix}") >= _INTACT_MIN_MATRICES:
        return text  # already intact - never overwrite a good export

    # Replace through the closing 💡 paragraph, but keep anything a later
    # answer may have appended after it.
    closing = _NEXT_STEPS_RE.search(text, match.end())
    end = closing.end() if closing else len(text)
    start = match.start()
    line_start = text.rfind("\n", 0, start) + 1
    if _HEADING_PREFIX_RE.fullmatch(text[line_start:start]):
        start = line_start
    return text[:start] + _EXTENDED_ANSWER.rstrip() + text[end:]
